<div class="accordion__item">
  <button class="accordion__header" aria-expanded="false" type="button">
    <span class="accordion__number">5</span>
    A roadmap to the reading
    <span class="accordion__chevron" aria-hidden="true">▼</span>
  </button>
  <div class="accordion__content">
    <div class="accordion__body">
<p>The four Questions we will read hang together as a unit on law, moving from the general to the specific.</p>
<p><strong>Question 90</strong> asks what law <em>is</em>. Aquinas works out a definition: law is an ordinance of reason, directed to the common good, made by someone with authority, and promulgated (made public). Each element matters, and each is argued for in its own Article.</p>
<p><strong>Question 91</strong> identifies the four kinds of law: eternal, natural, human, and divine. The eternal law is the plan of the divine mind itself. The natural law is our participation in the eternal law through our reason. Human law is what governments make, and it derives its authority from natural law. Divine law is what God reveals directly, through Scripture.</p>
<p><strong>Question 94</strong> zeroes in on natural law. Aquinas states its first precept, explains how further precepts follow from it, and takes up the hard questions: can natural law change, and is it the same for everyone? This is the heart of the unit.</p>
<p><strong>Question 95, Articles 1 and 2</strong> ask whether human law is useful at all, and whether an unjust human law is really a law. This is where Aquinas delivers his most famous line on law: <em>lex iniusta non est lex</em>, "an unjust law is no law."</p>
<p>Read each Question in order. The arguments build on each other, and later Questions assume the definitions from earlier ones.</p>
